From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 15:42:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: the shell gets the keyboard you configured

A tty run logged `No current XKB state` from
`XkbKeyboardLayoutEngine::XkbLookup` before every keypress, and that line is
the whole of a desktop nobody can type into.

`xkb_state_` is set by `XkbKeyboardLayoutEngine::SetKeymap` and by nothing
else. Its three callers are `SetCurrentLayoutByName` -- `#if
BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)` around a `NOTIMPLEMENTED()`, with
`CanSetCurrentLayout()` answering false to match -- `OnKeymapLoaded`, which is
only reachable from it, and `SetCurrentLayoutFromBuffer`, which is NOT gated
and whose one caller in the tree is `WaylandKeyboard::OnKeymap`, handling
`wl_keyboard.keymap` for the Wayland ozone platform. Domicile is a
non-ChromeOS DRM/Ozone build running neither, so the browser process holds a
null xkb state for its whole life.

WHY THAT LOOKED HARMLESS. `Lookup` still returns true: `XkbLookup` fails, and
the fallback is `DomCodeToNonPrintableDomKey`, a static table. Escape, the
function keys and Ctrl+Alt+F<n> go on decoding correctly out of it, which is
why the VT-switch chord was measured working. A printable key is not in that
table. It comes out as `DomKey::UNIDENTIFIED` with the US-QWERTY
`KeyboardCode` for wherever the key physically sits -- no `key`, no text, and
the user's `xkb_layout`/`xkb_variant` ignored in the browser process entirely.

THE KEYMAP IS THE COMPOSITOR'S, SO IT TRAVELS COMPOSITOR -> ENGINE. The
compositor compiles it from `input.keyboard` and hands it to every Wayland
client; sending that same text on to the browser is what keeps the shell and
the windows on it reading one layout rather than each reading that config for
itself, which is the class of disagreement patches 0018 and 0022 were. It
rides the channel that already runs in that direction -- the chrome control
socket, with the handshake, beside `displays` -- as
`{"type":"keymap","keymap":"..."}`.

`ControlChannel::DispatchLine` takes it before the `client_` gate and does not
relay it: the page has no use for 40 kilobytes of xkb, and the compositor has
already resolved the modifiers against it. The layout engine belongs to the UI
thread and this channel is read on the IO thread, so the binder -- which runs
on the UI thread -- binds `SetProcessKeymap` to it and hands the callback in.
That is what keeps `//components/domicile:control_channel` free of
`//content`.

`components/domicile/browser/keyboard_layout_unittest.cc` is the bug written
down: a keymap saying the key QWERTY prints `s` on is an `o`, an engine with
none decoding that key as `UNIDENTIFIED`/`VKEY_S`, and the same engine given
the keymap decoding it as `o`/`VKEY_O`.
---
 chrome/browser/BUILD.gn                       |  5 +++--
 .../chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc       | 21 +++++++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 22 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/chrome/browser/BUILD.gn b/chrome/browser/BUILD.gn
index 0670a8aa..53f18705 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/BUILD.gn
+++ b/chrome/browser/BUILD.gn
@@ -2384,10 +2384,11 @@ source_set("browser") {
     "//components/domain_reliability",
 
     # Domicile's engine fork: serving the shell over domicile://, the
-    # shell's control channel to the compositor, and the socket a running
-    # engine is told which shell to serve on.
+    # shell's control channel to the compositor, the keymap that channel
+    # carries, and the socket a running engine is told which shell to serve on.
     "//chrome/browser/domicile",
     "//components/domicile:control_channel",
+    "//components/domicile:keyboard_layout",
     "//components/domicile:shell_scheme",
     "//components/domicile:web_view_guest",
 
diff --git a/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc b/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc
index a45fafa1..0de51714 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc
@@ -4,7 +4,9 @@
 
 #include "chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.h"
 
+#include "base/task/bind_post_task.h"
 #include "components/domicile/browser/control_channel.h"
+#include "components/domicile/browser/keyboard_layout.h"
 #include "components/domicile/browser/web_view_guest.h"
 #include "content/public/browser/browser_thread.h"
 #include "mojo/public/cpp/bindings/message.h"
@@ -492,9 +494,24 @@ void PopulateChromeFrameBinders(
         // On the IO thread: the channel talks to a unix socket through
         // net::SocketPosix, which DCHECKs that it is on a thread with an IO
         // message pump. Binders run on the UI thread, so this has to hop.
+        //
+        // AND ONE MESSAGE ON THAT SOCKET HAS TO HOP BACK. The compositor's
+        // keymap is not the page's business and never reaches it: what it is
+        // for is this process's own KeyboardLayoutEngine, which off ChromeOS
+        // nothing else ever gives a keymap at all -- see
+        // components/domicile/browser/keyboard_layout.h for what that costs.
+        // That engine belongs to the UI thread, along with every evdev key
+        // dispatched through it, so the return trip is bound HERE, where the
+        // UI thread is already the current one. Reaching for it out there
+        // would work and would put //content into
+        // //components/domicile:control_channel, which deliberately has none.
         content::GetIOThreadTaskRunner({})->PostTask(
-            FROM_HERE, base::BindOnce(&domicile::BindControlChannel,
-                                      std::move(receiver)));
+            FROM_HERE,
+            base::BindOnce(
+                &domicile::BindControlChannel, std::move(receiver),
+                base::BindPostTask(
+                    content::GetUIThreadTaskRunner({}),
+                    base::BindRepeating(&domicile::SetProcessKeymap))));
       }));
 
   // Domicile's engine fork: a <webview> asking for a guest to put behind it.
